{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. ___. I'm Dr. ___ from the medicine team. I understand you've been having fevers and chills at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, doctor. Last night I spiked a fever up to 101.3. I was shaking so hard I couldn’t stop. Just felt awful."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:37:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Any cough, sore throat, or shortness of breath?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:38:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, nothing like that. No chest pain either. Just tired and shaky."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Any belly pain, nausea, or changes in urination?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, stomach feels fine. Bowels are normal. No burning when I pee."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. Given your history—liver transplant, diabetes, recent hernia surgery—we want to be careful. We’ve sent blood and urine tests to check for infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:42:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been on a lot of meds since the transplant. Rapamune, tacrolimus, Myfortic…"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:43:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and those help prevent rejection, but they also make you more vulnerable to infections. We’re also checking your kidney function and electrolytes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My kidneys have been a little off lately. Creatinine was around 2.3 before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right. Today it’s 3.0, so a bit higher. Could be from dehydration during the fever. We’re giving you fluids to help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, and my potassium came back high in the ER, didn’t it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it was 6.2, which is concerning. But your EKG was normal, and we gave you kayexalate and insulin to bring it down. It’s already improved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Could that be from my meds? I know tacrolimus can affect kidneys."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2180-01-21 11:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Both tacrolimus and the Bactrim you take for prevention can contribute. We’re watching closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2180-01-21 12:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your chest X-ray came back clear—no pneumonia. Stool test was negative for C. diff. No signs of infection so far."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2180-01-21 12:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what’s causing the fever then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2180-01-21 12:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Could be a virus. We’re not starting antibiotics unless we find something. Your exam is otherwise normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2180-01-21 12:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had a fever like this a few weeks ago. They thought it was viral then too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2180-01-21 12:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That fits. We’ll keep monitoring your temperature and repeat labs tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2180-01-21 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just checking in. How are you feeling now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2180-01-21 14:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "More tired than feverish now. Still a bit shaky, but no chills."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2180-01-21 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. You’re afebrile now, which is a positive sign. We’ll keep you hydrated and monitor your kidney numbers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2180-01-21 18:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Evening check-in. Your potassium is down to 5.3 and creatinine improved to 2.5. That’s trending in the right direction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2180-01-21 18:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good to hear. Should I stop any of my usual meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2180-01-21 18:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re holding your Lasix for now—don’t want to worsen the kidney issue. Otherwise, continue everything except we’re adjusting your transplant meds soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. Your labs this morning look better. Potassium is 4.2—normal. Creatinine is 2.4, still coming down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the fever?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve been afebrile all night. Blood cultures are still pending, but so far no growth. Likely viral."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my transplant meds? I was on both tacrolimus and sirolimus."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your sirolimus level came back at 6.7, which is therapeutic. So we’re stopping tacrolimus and increasing sirolimus to 4 mg daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Why stop tacrolimus?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Because it can affect kidneys and potassium. Since your sirolimus is now at a good level, we can rely on that instead."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. And Lasix?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2180-01-22 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re holding it for now. Restart it after your liver clinic visit. They’ll guide that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to discharge you today. You’re feeling better, labs are improving, and no fever."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. What do I need to do after I go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take sirolimus 4 mg daily—stop the tacrolimus. Continue Myfortic, Bactrim, and your other meds as listed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Get your sirolimus level checked next week and see the transplant clinic. Call to confirm the appointment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I be okay without tacrolimus?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. Sirolimus is now at a good level, and this change helps protect your kidneys. We’ll keep monitoring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks, doctor. I feel much better already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2180-01-22 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll discharge you in a few hours. Nurse will go over your meds and instructions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2180-01-22 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Final check before discharge. Any questions?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2180-01-22 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I think I’ve got it. Stop tacrolimus, increase sirolimus, hold Lasix, follow up next week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2180-01-22 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. You’re alert, walking fine, and feeling well. We’re discharging you home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2180-01-22 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Appreciate all the care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2180-01-22 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care, and call if you develop fever, swelling, or feel unwell."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2180-01-22 14:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. Thank you."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2180-01-21 11:33:00",
    "admission_end": "2180-01-22 15:13:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A male patient with a history of liver transplant, diabetes, and recent hernia repair was admitted for evaluation of fevers and chills at home. He was found to have hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury on chronic kidney disease, likely prerenal in origin due to reduced intake during illness. He was treated with kayexalate and IV fluids, with improvement in potassium and renal function. Infectious workup was negative, including normal chest X-ray, negative C. diff toxin, and no growth in urine culture; blood cultures later identified Enterobacter cloacae complex but were interpreted in the context of likely contamination. The fever resolved without antibiotics, presumed viral. Tacrolimus was discontinued and sirolimus increased to 4 mg daily given therapeutic levels and concern for tacrolimus-related nephrotoxicity. Lasix was held during admission. The patient was discharged home afebrile with follow-up arranged in the transplant clinic and instructions to monitor sirolimus levels.",
    "problems": [
      "Unspecified viral infection",
      "Acute kidney failure, unspecified",
      "Hyperpotassemia",
      "Liver replaced by transplant",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, uncontrolled",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Diaphragmatic hernia without mention of obstruction or gangrene",
      "Dyskinesia of esophagus",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Insomnia, unspecified",
      "Hypertensive chronic kidney disease, unspecified, with chronic kidney disease stage I through stage IV, or unspecified",
      "Chronic kidney disease, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
